<h1>Nick Chubb <em>Career Athletic Profile</em></h1>
<div class="hero-meta">
<span class="hero-meta-item">📅 Updated August 23, 2026</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">🏈 Cleveland Browns · Houston Texans</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">📊 2018 Combine Measurements</span>
</div>
<p class="hero-intro">
    Nick Chubb retired on August 21, 2026 with 7,349 rushing yards, 59 total touchdowns, and one of the most efficient careers of his generation — 5.03 yards per carry across 1,462 attempts. His 2018 combine numbers, run just months after tearing knee ligaments at Georgia, are worth revisiting: a 4.52-second 40-yard dash, a 38.5-inch vertical, and 29 reps on the bench press. Eight seasons later, almost every number held up exactly as advertised.
  </p>
</div><div class="article-wrap">
<div class="content">
<h2>The 2018 Combine Numbers</h2>
<div class="player-card">
<div class="player-name">Nick Chubb</div>
<div class="player-sub">Georgia · Running Back · 2018 NFL Draft, Round 2, Pick 35</div>
<div class="player-stats">
<div class="player-stat above">
<div class="player-stat-val">4.52s</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">40-Yard Dash</div>
</div>
<div class="player-stat above">
<div class="player-stat-val">38.5"</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Vertical Jump</div>
</div>
<div class="player-stat above">
<div class="player-stat-val">10'8"</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Broad Jump</div>
</div>
<div class="player-stat above">
<div class="player-stat-val">29 reps</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Bench Press (225 lbs)</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Chubb measured 5-foot-11 and 227 pounds at the 2018 combine, then ran a 4.52-second 40-yard dash with a 1.56-second 10-yard split — the sixth-fastest time among running backs that year. His 38.5-inch vertical and 10-foot-8 broad jump both ranked near the top of his position group, and his 29 reps on the bench press tied Saquon Barkley for the most among all running backs in attendance.</p>
<p>What made the performance remarkable wasn't just where the numbers ranked — it was the context. Chubb was less than three years removed from a gruesome knee ligament injury at Georgia in 2015, the kind that ends careers before they start. He didn't just recover; he tested as one of the most explosive backs in his draft class doing it.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">🦵</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>The injury context matters:</strong> Chubb tore multiple ligaments in his knee during a 2015 game against Tennessee — the kind of injury that historically depresses combine testing even years later, if a player tests again at all. Posting a 38.5-inch vertical and a sub-4.6 40 on that knee wasn't just good for a running back; it was a sign his rebuilt knee had fully returned to elite-level function, something teams weighed heavily in a draft class led by Saquon Barkley.</div>
</div>
