<div class="rank-track"><div class="rank-fill" style="width:95%;background:#aaff00"></div></div>
<div class="rank-val">4th all-time (1,000+ carries)</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Chubb's combine explosiveness translated almost directly onto the field. He debuted with 5.2 yards per carry as a rookie and stayed above 5.0 in each of his first six NFL seasons — a level of efficiency his combine testing had already hinted was possible. He finishes his career as just the fourth running back in NFL history with both 1,000-plus carries and a 5.0-yard-per-carry average, joining Jim Brown, Joe Perry, and Jamaal Charles — a list that spans nearly seven decades of the sport.</p>
<h2>The Career, By the Numbers</h2>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Category</th>
<th>Career Total</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="highlight"><td>Rushing Yards</td><td>7,349</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rushing Attempts</td><td>1,462</td></tr>
<tr><td>Yards Per Carry</td><td>5.03</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rushing Touchdowns</td><td>54</td></tr>
<tr><td>Receptions / Receiving Yards</td><td>141 / 1,109</td></tr>
<tr><td>Total Touchdowns</td><td>59</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pro Bowl Selections</td><td>4 (2019–2022)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Games Played / Started</td><td>100 / 87</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Chubb rushed for over 1,000 yards in four consecutive seasons from 2019 through 2022, peaking at a career-high 1,525 yards and 12 touchdowns in 2022, which earned him Second-team All-Pro honors. A knee injury just two games into the 2023 season — his second major knee injury after the one at Georgia — interrupted his prime. He returned partway through 2024 with the Browns before signing with the Houston Texans for a final season in 2025, where he split carries with rookie Woody Marks before announcing his retirement on Instagram at age 30.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">🏆</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>"There's a life waiting for you after football, too":</strong> In his retirement announcement, Chubb emphasized ending his career healthy after a winning season, rather than pushing through for statistics. His Hall of Fame case is genuinely debated — a brilliant but injury-shortened prime measured against the longer resumes of the position's inner circle — but his per-carry efficiency across 1,462 attempts puts him in company with some of the most productive runners the position has ever seen.</div>
</div>
